      "writtenOn": "2018-08-28T20:23:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ok, this is in how python works. All changes to instances get stored in the self.__dict__, so what we have done is created a dictionary on the class, called __shared_object_state, we then rebind the instances __dict__ to the class attribute __shared_object_state.\n\nthis means that any change we do to the instance is applied to the class __dict__ [and since all instances share the __dict__ change to one instance impacts all instances in memory]\n\nThe __setattr__ is strictly used (in super form) so no one accidentally sets values we don\u0027t expect.\n\nLong term this allows us to instantiate on demand instead of needing to maintain a global ProvidersAPIs, long term.\n\nSo basically what happens here:\n\nObjectX (with rebound __dict__) is instantiated\n\nobjectX.NewAttr \u003d True\n\nObjectY instantiated (same class as ObjectX), and if you set NewAttr \u003d False, both instances reflect the change.",
